    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9fa6873e_8f6becb3",
        "filename": "common/timesync.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 93,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-06T20:35:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This isn\u0027t really my domain, but I worry that this may be too slow with all the soft division operations, at least if we ever actually want to put it into every CPRITNF().\n\nHow about doing something like this:\n\n{\n uint32_t current_secs \u003d get_time() / SECOND;\n uint32_t diff_secs \u003d current_secs - local_epoch.local_secs;\n\n if (unlikely(diff_secs \u003e 60 - local_epoch.rtc_tm.second)) {\n   if (unlikely(diff_secs \u003e 120 - local_epoch.rtc_tm.second ||\n                local_epoch.rtc_tm.minute \u003d\u003d 59)) { // rare slow paths\n     local_epoch.rtc_tm \u003d sec_to_date(date_to_sec(local_epoch.rtc_tm) + diff_secs);\n   } else {\n     local_epoch.rtc_tm.second +\u003d diff_secs - 60\n     local_epoch.rtc_tm.minute++;\n } else {\n   local_epoch.rtc_tm.second +\u003d diff_secs;\n }\n\n local_epoch.local_secs \u003d current_secs;\n return local_epoch.rtc_tm;\n}\n\nIn order to do this efficiently you\u0027d want to store the RTC sync point as a struct calendar_date, not an epoch second, so maybe that means we should just transfer the timestamp in that format from coreboot right away, because right now we\u0027d just be converting back and forth a bunch of times for no reason.",
      "revId": "8c76553b1f711480b853340be892bae8fe6be649",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "791c6a35_0dcb51c2",
        "filename": "common/timesync.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 93,
      "author": {
        "id": 1327490
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-06T21:04:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s an interesting idea.  Right now, the code is only called from the HOOK_CHIPSET_* hooks.  I think that change makes more sense if we do want to integrate it into CPRINTF, for example.  For the purposes it\u0027s used for right now, sec_to_date is still probably going to be called every time.  I\u0027m thinking of maybe filing a Feature Request for that change and see if there\u0027s enough interest in it.  Thoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "9fa6873e_8f6becb3",
      "revId": "8c76553b1f711480b853340be892bae8fe6be649",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "591797c1_29a8867f",
        "filename": "common/timesync.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 93,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002133
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-06T21:09:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, it would be useful to decide now if it does have an impact on what the ideal format for the sync message between coreboot and the EC is, because we don\u0027t want to have to change that after we started shipping things. I do think putting it in CPRINTF would be a great convenience feature (and shouldn\u0027t really cost anything with a careful implementation like suggested above), but I don\u0027t know how the EC people feel about it. (I did my fair share of EC log reconstruction on Scarlet, and having to manually add up seconds and then transform epoch back into dates everywhere certainly was a bit of a chore.)",
      "parentUuid": "791c6a35_0dcb51c2",
      "revId": "8c76553b1f711480b853340be892bae8fe6be649",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
